<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Nature’s Wonders</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's a Slow, Furry Sloth!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Summarize short stories using drawings or pictures. Communication: Relay simple information received verbally using English or Spanish.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can summarize short stories using drawings or pictures (e.g., draw a slow sloth to show the story's main idea). Can relay simple information received verbally by pointing to images (e.g., points to "furry animal" on a picture of a sloth). Can describe new vocabulary in a story using drawing and pictures (e.g., describing the rainforest showing a picture).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and shows pictures of rainforest animals (sloth, monkey, toucan). The teacher says, "Look! What animal is it?" and points. Students call out names. The teacher focuses on the sloth, saying, "It's a slow, furry sloth!" and makes a slow movement. Then, the teacher shows a picture of a rainforest and asks, "Where does the sloth live?" Students answer, "Rainforest!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a sloth and says clearly, "This is a sloth. It is slow and furry." The teacher then draws a simple sloth on the board and points to the drawing, repeating, "Slow sloth, furry sloth." The teacher models the pronunciation of /s/ in "sloth" and /r/ in "rainforest" by over-articulating the sounds and asking students to repeat. For /dʒ/ in "jungle," the teacher shows a "jungle" picture and clearly says the word, emphasizing the initial sound.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of a fast rabbit and asks, "Is the sloth fast?" (Expected answer: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the sloth picture and asks, "Is it a bird?" (Expected answer: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the furry part of the sloth and asks, "Is it furry?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Discovering Nature’s Wonders"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated story about a sloth in the rainforest, presented on large flashcards or a projector. The story includes target vocabulary (rainforest, sloth, leaf, climb, sleep) and grammar (adjectives + nouns, present simple). Example text: "This is a slow sloth. It lives in the rainforest. It eats green leaves. It sleeps on a branch. The sloth is furry."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpreting and exchanging simple information about familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to pictures. Then asks, "What is the story about?" (Expected: "Sloth!") "Where does it live?" (Expected: "Rainforest!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (TPR):</b> Teacher makes a statement, students stand up for "True" or sit down for "False." E.g., "The sloth flies fast." (False). "The sloth eats leaves." (True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Teacher shows pictures of a sloth, a leaf, and a branch. Teacher says, "What does the sloth eat?" Students point to the leaf picture. "Where does it sleep?" Students point to the branch.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Summarizing short stories using drawings/pictures; relaying simple information using adjectives + nouns (e.g., slow sloth, furry animal), WH-questions ("What animal is it?"), Present simple ("It's the rainforest."), and target vocabulary (rainforest, sloth, leaf, branch, climb, sleep).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Story Picture Sequencing & Labeling:</b> The teacher provides students with printed strips of paper, each showing a key picture from the short sloth story (e.g., sloth, sloth eating a leaf, sloth sleeping on a branch). Students cut out the pictures and glue them in order in a notebook. Below each picture, they write 1-2 words (e.g., "slow sloth," "eats leaves," "sleeps branch") or choose from given word options to label the main idea of each picture. This activity helps students identify and represent key story elements visually and with minimal text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Describing Pictures:</b> The teacher shows a flashcard of a rainforest animal (e.g., a monkey, a toucan, a frog). The teacher prompts with sentence frames: "This is a ___ (animal). It is ___ (adjective)." Students complete the sentences orally, first chorally, then individually. For example, "This is a monkey. It is jumpy." or "This is a colorful toucan." The teacher ensures correct use of adjectives + nouns.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "Tell a Friend" Activity:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a simple drawing of a rainforest animal (e.g., one gets a sloth, the other a parrot). Student A points to their drawing and says, "This is a furry sloth. It is slow." Student B listens and then draws a simple picture of what Student A described. Then they switch roles. If Student B doesn't understand, Student A can point to their original drawing or use Spanish to clarify (e.g., "Es un oso perezoso. Lento."). This relays information verbally and tests comprehension through drawing.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Discovering Nature’s Wonders"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Project 2: Nature Storybook – Page Creation and Explanation.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will continue working on their "Nature Storybook." Today, they will create one or two pages, focusing on summarizing a simple fact or a short action sequence about a rainforest animal (e.g., the sloth) using drawings and very short descriptive sentences. The teacher provides paper and drawing materials.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reminds students of the sloth story and other rainforest animals discussed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose one animal (e.g., sloth, monkey, toucan) and one simple action or characteristic (e.g., "The sloth is slow," "The monkey climbs," "The toucan is colorful").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students draw a picture depicting their chosen animal and action/characteristic for their storybook page. (e.g., drawing a sloth moving very slowly, or a monkey swinging on vines).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below their drawing, students write a very simple sentence, guided by the teacher on the board (e.g., "This is a slow sloth." "It eats leaves." "The monkey is jumpy."). The teacher circulates, providing individual support for spelling and sentence structure.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In small groups (3-4 students), each student shows their storybook page to their group members. They point to their drawing and relay the simple information verbally, "This is a slow sloth. It sleeps," or "This is a jumpy monkey." They can also point to parts of their drawing to explain.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "My Nature Storybook Page Explanation."</b> Students present their completed storybook page to the teacher or a peer. They point to their drawing and verbally summarize/describe its content using 1-2 simple sentences. They may also point to key elements in their drawing to describe new vocabulary if needed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can summarize story information using drawings (drawing clearly shows main idea).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can relay simple information verbally (e.g., "This is a slow sloth." / "It eats leaves.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (sloth, rainforest, leaf, furry, slow).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses adjectives + nouns (e.g., "slow sloth," "green leaf").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation is intelligible (especially /s/ and /r/ sounds).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students draw a happy face if they can tell about their animal using drawings and words, or a thinking face if they need more practice. They can also point to a flashcard: "Easy" or "Hard."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompts:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What animal did you draw today? Can you tell me one thing about it?" (Teacher asks verbally as students pack up).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or hard to draw and talk about your animal?" (Students show thumbs up/down).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? (e.g., Students effectively used drawings to summarize and relay simple facts about rainforest animals, demonstrating their understanding of descriptive language).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson/theme? (e.g., Provide more sentence frames or word banks for students struggling with verbal relay. Encourage peer-to-peer feedback on drawing clarity to enhance mediation through visuals).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "It's a Slow, Furry Sloth!" theme. The next theme will introduce new animals and habitats, allowing students to continue developing their ability to summarize and relay information about nature's wonders.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>